#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553463 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553463 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b648352b-9cfb-5d5c-8ea0-b1cb8e46e03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553472 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d3273a24-dde6-50e8-9d77-b0d57e06622e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,6)=0.8,\\,F(8,2)=0.5,\\,F(2,6)=0.4,\\,F(2,2)=0.2.\n$$\n\nFind $P(2\\le X\\le 8,2\\le Y\\le 6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "392c7844-059b-541a-9161-f7e9d9b52570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553516 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/15/1524afeeaea3cc0fd4ee51b9572222bdd846fba378e029e4fa20a272a4fe6353.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(8,2)$ and $(2,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 8,2\\le Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(8,6)-F(8,2)-F(2,6)+F(2,2) \\\\  & =0.8-0.5-0.4+0.2 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64924153-c273-5495-a9b7-e1268d0994d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553530 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/00/00e0dcbd7ea22f09db76b03fc4d7bda328f1b1f17ceeb998cf039241dafd8da3.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{4}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v{\\int }_{1}^{x}u\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{2}-1){\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{2}-1)[\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}, & -1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<-1,\\,y>2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e8f43e3-d7f0-593f-9f61-7957ca2c311e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553542 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1,\\,y>2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/04/04eee4bcf3263318946503732ed7715913e12949659cacb84e93d1696ee7f2e1.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}{y}^{2}, & 2\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<1,\\,-4<y<1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca240843-51e6-5695-9d1f-64a8db44b027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553570 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<1,\\,-4<y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0c/0c027a6d656653ee1158bdff681405afe99748d734555f515de563099146849e.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "191a9709-ec5c-5263-bac1-283c8d8f7000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69db2255-e176-5209-8d02-619b8b9048bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b1b9c05-8e73-5803-a166-04d6b3142c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2ed5b00-2fd7-55f9-9dbf-26e347814268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553530 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
